From bfd3d1757e7c891333eb505b7c00e501510ca7ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Stufflebeam <cpuchip@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:18:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] 055 (2/5): STU_WASM, WebAssembly apps through WAMR, off
 by default

With STU_WASM=1 in the environment, the stand-in and Rev A builds add WAMR's ESP-IDF component from build/wamr
(1/5) and firmware/wasm/wasm_app.{c,h}, the bridge between the framework's app_def calls and a game built with
stu32-arcade sdk/wasm/guest.c (from stu32-arcade sdk/wasm/bridge.c at aeb8fca). Off, nothing of it is built.
- device/sdkconfig.wasm: WAMR's AOT loader only; no interpreter, libc layers or pthread library. It is appended
  to the caller's SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS, never replacing them (soroban #5542).
- WAMR_NO_LWIP (patch 0005): no libc-wasi, so no lwip and the VFS and console drivers it would bring.
- WAMR_BUILD_QUICK_AOT_ENTRY=0: its 768 B table is sorted at init, so it would sit in internal DRAM; the generic
  call path costs about 0.2% of a frame (BABLW 381.0 frames a second off, 381.7 on).
- The bridge: WAMR is initialised at a game's load and destroyed at its stop, so it holds nothing while the
  calculator runs. Its allocations and the module's copy go to PSRAM (a running game: 152 B of internal heap,
  104 B of it once, at the first wasm_runtime_full_init). It sets the calc task's native stack boundary itself,
  2 KB above the stack's start, since WAMR's own lookup returns NULL without the trace facility. Under the
  frame guard (-Werror=frame-larger-than=1024).
- run.sh and build.sh build it in build-wasm and run fetch-wamr.sh; run.sh re-makes the sdkconfig when
  device/sdkconfig.wasm is newer, like 051's rule for sdkconfig.defaults.
- make clang-check compiles the bridge with -DSTU_WASM and WAMR's headers when build/wamr is fetched, and says
  so when it is not.

Checked: resources.py static ab51544 against this commit, option off, is +0 on both targets (see 055's work
file in 5/5 for the table with the option on).

Written by arcade for soroban's gate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
